// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(5): "A destructor,
// allocation function, or deallocation function shall not be declared
// `consteval`." This is the exact rule [dcl.constexpr]/2 states -- "A
// destructor, an allocation function, or a deallocation function shall not be
// declared with the consteval specifier" -- restated normatively in the scpp
// spec, and it is the boundary of the permissive reading used by
// consteval_static_member_function_call_folds.scpp and
// consteval_member_function_call_on_named_object_folds.scpp: ordinary member
// functions are absent from the list and are therefore allowed, while
// destructors are named and are therefore not.
//
// `struct` is used deliberately: docs/spec/en/11-inheritance-and-interfaces.md
// (ch11) §11.5(1) requires every *class* to declare a virtual destructor, and
// §11.1(2.3) forbids a `struct` from declaring one, so `struct` is the only
// construct in which a plain, non-virtual destructor can be written -- which
// isolates the §9.1(5) violation from any virtual-destructor rule. §11.1(4)
// confirms a `struct` may otherwise declare members "exactly as the ordinary
// C++ rules permit".
//
// Rejection independently confirmed with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which
// reports: "error: destructor cannot be declared consteval".
struct Box {
    int value;

    consteval ~Box() {
        return;
    }
};

int main() {
    return 0;
}
